      <p class="m3-pl-row__body">Most solar control films are summer products. 3M Thinsulate Climate Control Series is different - it's a dual-season low-emissivity film line engineered to work in both directions. In summer, the series rejects solar heat gain. In winter, it reflects interior warmth back into the room rather than letting it escape through the glass. The result is a window that performs closer to double-pane insulated glass without the cost or disruption of replacement. The series flagship, CC75, improves a single-pane window's U-value from 1.03 to 0.62 - a roughly 40% improvement that's meaningful in Philadelphia's IECC Zone 4A winters where January averages dip to 25-35°F.</p>
      <p class="m3-pl-row__body">Thinsulate films maintain high visible light transmission - the CC75 comes in at 74% VLT and reads as clear glass from inside and out. Unlike reflective metallized films, Thinsulate won't give your home a mirrored exterior appearance. That matters in Philadelphia's historic neighborhoods: Germantown, Chestnut Hill, Society Hill, and Fairmount all have HOA or historic commission guidelines that restrict high-reflectance coatings. With just 15% exterior reflectance, Thinsulate stays well within those limits while delivering meaningful energy performance in every season.</p>

