      <span class="m3-pl-row__eyebrow">3MTM Daylight Redirecting Film</span>
      <h3 class="m3-pl-row__heading">Daylight Redirecting Film — Natural light, deeper into every room</h3>
      <p class="m3-pl-row__body">Most window films control what comes in. Daylight Redirecting Film (DRF) does something different - it redirects incoming sunlight upward toward the ceiling, distributing natural daylight up to approximately 40 feet from the window. The film's microstructured prismatic surface bends light using total internal reflection, routing it along the ceiling plane where it diffuses evenly into the room. The result is dramatically better daylighting across the entire floor plate - not just the perimeter seats - without glare and without heat gain.</p>
      <p class="m3-pl-row__body">For Philadelphia's Center City office buildings - many of which have deep floor plates where interior workstations receive almost no natural light - DRF meaningfully reduces dependence on artificial lighting during daylight hours. The film qualifies for LEED daylighting credits and pairs well with automated lighting controls: as DRF brings more daylight deeper into the space, occupancy and daylight sensors can dim overhead fixtures in response, compounding the energy savings. Best results on south- and east-facing glass with clear or lightly tinted existing glazing. DRF is installed on the interior surface and maintains approximately 80% visible light transmission.</p>

