      <h2 class="m3-casestudy__title">How 3M Daylight Redirecting Film helped power a net-zero retail store</h2>
      <p class="m3-casestudy__lead">When Walgreens opened its first net-zero energy store in Evanston, Illinois, one of its key challenges was lighting - specifically, how to reduce dependence on electric lighting when natural light only penetrates a few feet past the window line.</p>
      <p class="m3-casestudy__body">The solution was 3M Daylight Redirecting Film installed across 4,000 square feet of west-facing curtain wall. The film's microstructured surface redirects incoming sunlight upward toward the ceiling rather than letting it fall to the floor near the windows, spreading natural daylight up to 40 feet into the store interior. By bouncing up to 80% of that light off the ceiling, the store significantly reduced its need for artificial lighting during daylight hours - a critical factor in hitting net-zero energy targets, since lighting accounts for roughly 40% of electricity use in commercial buildings.</p>
      <p class="m3-casestudy__body">The same physics apply to any Philadelphia commercial property with a deep floor plate and significant glazing. Center City office buildings, Walnut Street retailers, and Old City restaurants and hospitality spaces all share the same problem: great glass on the perimeter, but dark interior workspaces that run on overhead lighting all day. 3M Daylight Redirecting Film is one of the few interventions that actually solves that problem at the glass - without construction, without replacing windows, and while qualifying for LEED daylighting credits.</p>
      <blockquote class="m3-casestudy__quote">
        <p>&ldquo;3M Daylight Redirecting Film was the perfect fit for our 4,000 sq ft west-facing curtain wall - a cost-effective, low-maintenance solution to reduce visible glare and extend our daylight zone.&rdquo;</p>
        <footer>Jason Robbins &mdash; Manager of Mechanical Engineering, Walgreens</footer>
