<section class="m3-faq" id="faq">
  <div class="m3-faq__inner">
    <div class="m3-faq__header fade-in">
      <span class="m3-eyebrow">Questions</span>
      <h2 class="m3-faq__title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <p>Honest answers about 3M window film in Philadelphia from specialists with over 16 years of installation experience.</p>
      <a href="/contact/" class="m3-faq__cta-btn">Talk to a Specialist</a>
    </div>
    <div class="m3-faq__list fade-in">
      <div class="m3-faq__item m3-faq__item--open">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>What 3M window film products does Window Film Philadelphia install?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>We install the full 3M architectural window film lineup, including Sun Control (solar/energy), Thinsulate Climate Control (insulation), Scotchshield Safety &amp; Security, Fasara Decorative, Daylight Redirecting, and Night Vision films. We carry both residential-grade and commercial-grade products for every application and budget.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>How long does 3M window film last, and does it come with a warranty?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>3M window films are designed to last 10-20+ years depending on the product, glass type, and sun exposure. All 3M films installed by Window Film Philadelphia include a manufacturer-backed warranty covering film performance, adhesion, and color stability. Residential installations typically carry a lifetime warranty; commercial installations vary by product series.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>What is the difference between 3M Sun Control and 3M Thinsulate?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>3M Sun Control film is primarily a summer product - it rejects solar heat to reduce cooling costs and glare. 3M Thinsulate Climate Control film is engineered for year-round performance, adding an insulating layer that retains interior heat in winter while still rejecting solar heat in summer. For Philadelphia's four-season climate, Thinsulate often delivers a better year-round ROI, while Sun Control is ideal for high-sun southern and western exposures.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>Can 3M window film be applied to double-pane insulated glass?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>Yes - 3M makes specific film series designed for insulated glass units (IGUs). Selecting the wrong film for double-pane glass can cause thermal stress and seal failure, which is why professional assessment matters. Our team evaluates your glass type, orientation, and goals before recommending a film, ensuring you stay within 3M's approved specifications and don't void your window manufacturer's warranty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>How does 3M Daylight Redirecting film work, and where is it most useful?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>3M Daylight Redirecting film uses a microstructured prismatic surface to redirect incoming sunlight upward toward the ceiling, distributing natural light deep into a room - up to 40 feet from the window. This reduces the need for artificial lighting near window walls and dramatically improves daylighting in spaces like open-plan offices, classrooms, and healthcare facilities. It's particularly effective on south- and west-facing facades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>How quickly will I see a return on investment from 3M window film?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>Commercial properties with large glazing areas typically see full ROI within 2–3 years through energy savings alone. Residential properties typically see ROI within 3–5 years. For security films like Scotchshield, the ROI calculation also includes avoided theft losses, reduced insurance premiums (in some cases), and deferred glass replacement costs. Our team can provide a customized projection during your free consultation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>Is 3M window film worth it for Philadelphia winters?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>Yes — particularly for Philadelphia's large stock of aging single-pane rowhome glass and older commercial buildings with minimal glazing insulation. 3M Thinsulate Climate Control film reduces winter heat loss by adding an insulating layer without replacing the window. In IECC Climate Zone 4A, where heating loads are significant from November through March, Thinsulate-installed windows can improve winter U-value by up to 35%. This makes it one of the few window film products that genuinely pays for itself in both heating and cooling seasons.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>How much does 3M window film cost in Philadelphia?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>3M window film pricing in Philadelphia depends on the series, glass area, and installation complexity. Solar control films (Prestige Series) typically range from $8–$14 per square foot installed. Thinsulate Climate Control runs $10–$16 per square foot due to the precision application required. Scotchshield Safety &amp; Security film ranges from $9–$18 per square foot depending on thickness and any attachment systems required. Decorative Fasara films start around $6 per square foot for standard patterns. All pricing includes site assessment, professional installation, and the manufacturer warranty. Contact us for a free, no-obligation estimate specific to your property.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>Can 3M window film qualify for LEED or energy efficiency credits?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>Yes. Several 3M window film products are eligible for LEED v4 credits under the Daylight and Views category (for daylight-redirecting and glare-control films) and the Energy &amp; Atmosphere category (for solar control and insulating films). 3M Daylight Redirecting Film specifically can contribute to LEED daylighting credits. For commercial projects in Philadelphia pursuing LEED certification, we can provide NFRC-certified product specifications and installation documentation required for the credit submission. Ask about this during your free consultation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="m3-faq__item">
        <div class="m3-faq__q"><span>Will 3M window film work on historic or older Philadelphia windows?</span><span class="m3-faq__toggle">+</span></div>
        <div class="m3-faq__a"><p>3M window film can be applied to most single-pane historic glass, including wavy antique glass common in Philadelphia rowhomes and historic commercial buildings. For double-pane insulated glass units (IGUs) — common in mid-century and modern renovations — 3M makes specific film series rated for IGU application. The key issue is thermal stress: applying the wrong film to certain glass types can void window warranties or cause seal failure in IGUs. Our team always performs a glass type and orientation assessment before recommending a film, ensuring compatibility with your specific windows, whether they're 1890 rowhouse glass or modern curtain wall panels.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

